# Field-app quality

Outdoor WISP toolkit. Readability and working tools beat decoration.

## Contrast and gloves

- Text and status must read in sun. No pale grey-on-blue, no pastel chips as the only status cue. Pair color with shape or label.
- Hit targets ≥ `@dimen/tap_min` (48dp). Primary actions on the thumb edge.
- Night theme must be actually dark. Day / high-contrast must stay distinct. Keep all three working.

## Reuse chrome, do not fork it

- Buttons: `Widget.TowerScope.Button.Primary` / `.Secondary` / `.Ghost` in `styles.xml`. Do not invent a fourth button component.
- Preserve `android:id` values Kotlin binds, or update the Activity in the same change.
- Package `com.towerscope.ar`. Kotlin + XML. `minSdk 33`. No Compose unless one screen is justified.
- Do not break site import (KML/KMZ/CSV), location permissions, compass, or LOS math.

## How to run

- Open this folder in Android Studio, sync Gradle, run the `app` configuration on a **physical device** (GPS + magnetometer). Emulator is OK for layout screenshots only.
- `./gradlew :app:assembleDebug` for a compile check.
- Sideload notes and LOS API: `README.md`.

## Visual identity stays out of this rule

Taste, palettes, and “make it unique” live in `.cursor/skills/visual-redesign/SKILL.md`. Do not lock in the current card grid from this rule.
